Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (f) During the Pre-Closing Period, the Company Board may execute a Company Adverse Recommendation Change <omitted> if, and only if, <omitted> (iii) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel, that the failure to make the Company Adverse Recommendation Change or terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (iv) the Company has notified Parent of the Company Board’s intention to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change or terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the fifth Business Day after Parent receives such notice and (v) if not due to an Intervening Event pursuant to Section 9.3(g), the decision to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change is related to an Acquisition Proposal or the Company’s intent to terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement, and the Company has complied with clauses (1) through (6), as follows: (1) the Company Board shall have determined that such Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, (2) the Company shall have disclosed to Parent the material terms and conditions of such Acquisition Proposal and all related communications, (3) the Company shall have negotiated in good faith with Parent, to the extent Parent wishes to negotiate, during the five Business Day period provided in the foregoing clause (iv) of this Section 9.3(f) regarding proposed revisions to this Agreement, (4) after considering the outcomes of negotiations with Parent and taking into account the proposals made by Parent, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, the Company Board shall have determined in good faith that such Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal, and, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, that the failure to make the Company Adverse Recommendation Change or terminate this Agreement would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (5) the Company Board, after consultation with strategic advisors, concludes that the Acquisition Proposal aligns better with the Company’s long-term strategic goals and shareholder interests, and (6) if the Company intends to terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement, the Company shall have complied with Section 10.1(d)(i). For clarity, the provisions of this Section 9.3(f) shall also apply to any amendment to the financial terms or any other material amendment to any Acquisition Proposal (except that any reference to five Business Days shall instead be three Business Days) or any successive Acquisition Proposals. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer:
